[{:db/id 17592186096795 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ecbf1f26-9acd-560e-9d67-c86b72436e88" "question-998e5982-d7a4-5c3c-80a4-fa9e89a83e69" "question-affca196-0a76-5ce7-a774-751bad763eba" "question-45ac4819-8cd9-51ec-b2a8-2a52c6be8c3d"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186096604 :question/problem "Consider the following geometric sequence:\n\n$$\n6, \\quad -2, \\quad \\dfrac{2}{3}, \\quad -\\dfrac{2}{9}, \\quad \\ldots\n$$\n\nDetermine whether the sequence is convergent or divergent. If it converges, find the limit." :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term $a_1$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r| <    1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r| > 1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $a_1$\n\n- if $r = -1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nThe  given sequence is a geometric sequence with the common ratio \n\n$$\nr=\\dfrac{a_2}{a_1}=\\dfrac{(-2)}{6}= -\\dfrac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nSince $|r| = \\dfrac{1}{3} <      1,$ the sequence converges to $0.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186096796 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-5a93c245-9a12-5bd5-b2e3-6bc70e922035" "question-b1e6aead-9808-569e-a808-627f1ba618d2" "question-930eb473-5d0d-5364-be4e-fbc222527d17" "question-77ce968b-96fd-5748-9187-a2257efb0090" "question-9262426c-0414-546e-b699-b0af1ea969b4"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186096605 :question/problem "Does the sequence $a_n=\\left( \\dfrac{5}{3}\\right)^{n-1}$ for $n\\geq  1$ \nconverge or diverge? If it converges, what is its limit?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term $a_1$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r| <      1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r| > 1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $a_1$\n\n- if $r = -1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nThe given sequence $a_n=\\left( \\dfrac{5}{3}\\right)^{n-1}$ is a geometric sequence with the common ratio $r =\\dfrac{5}{3}.$ \n\nSince $|r| >   1,$ the sequence is divergent." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186096744 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ef42a7ee-2955-5cbb-8a98-8858f689ce0e" "question-3d43ba6e-0f3b-545b-8a27-3e657530e6fe" "question-6fc6aa47-4c64-51fa-b318-3ae3f17b8883" "question-f1a23838-d1bb-516b-9ae9-359aec1587f6"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186096551 :question/problem "Consider the following sequence: \n\n$$\na_{n+1} = -2a_n, \\qquad a_1 = -4, \\qquad n\\geq 1.\n$$\n\nDetermine whether the sequence is convergent or divergent. If it converges, find the limit." :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term $a_1$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r| <      1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r| > 1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $a_1$\n\n- if $r= -1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nThe given sequence is a geometric sequence with the common ratio \n\n$$\nr=\\dfrac{a_{n+1}}{a_n}= -2.\n$$\n\nSince $|r| >1$, the sequence diverges." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186097110 :question/problem "Consider the following geometric sequence:\n\n$$\n5,\\,-5,\\,5,\\,-5,\\,…\n$$\n\nDetermine whether the sequence is convergent or divergent. If it converges, find the limit." :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $|r|=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nThe  given sequence is a geometric sequence with the common ratio \n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{-5}{5}=-1.\n$$\n\nSince $r=-1,$ i.e., the elements alternate between $5$ and $-5,$ the sequence diverges." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186094633 :answer-field/correct 17592186094634 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-ecbf1f26-9acd-560e-9d67-c86b72436e88" :question/id #uuid "ecbf1f26-9acd-560e-9d67-c86b72436e88" :question/math-academy-id "q-141392" :question/problem "Consider the following geometric sequence:\n\n$$\n-3,\\,6,\\,-12,\\,24,\\,…\n$$\n\nDetermine whether the sequence is convergent or divergent. If it converges, find the limit." :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nThe  given sequence is a geometric sequence with the common ratio \n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{6}{(-3)}=-2.\n$$\n\nSince $|r|=2>1,$ the sequence diverges." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1c1316c7-31d9-554c-8efa-84778111277c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1c1316c7-31d9-554c-8efa-84778111277c" :answer-field/id #uuid "1c1316c7-31d9-554c-8efa-84778111277c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f3b59ff3-992c-5bee-97c7-f5563634ac94" :answer-field/choices ["answer-796d7024-a1e9-5